{"text": "SSF cn s'ScHUTZ\n\n1899 L Street, NW\nSuite 200\nWashington, DC 20036\n(202) 508-1100 | Phone\n(202) 861-9888 | Fax\n\nLee Levine\n(202) 508-1110\nllevine@lskslaw.com\n\nJanuary 9, 2013\n\nAttorney General Irvin B. Nathan\nDistrict of Columbia Attorney General\n441 4th Street, N.W.\nWashington, DC 20001\n\nDear Attorney General Nathan:\n\nI write in response to your invitation to Meet the Press to provide to you information\nconcerning the display of a large capacity firearm magazine during its December 23, 2012\nprogram. As you are aware, the magazine was displayed during David Gregory\u2019s interview with\nWayne LaPierre, the executive vice president and chief executive officer of the National Rifle\nAssociation. The interview included a discussion of the availability of large capacity magazines,\nwhich permit multiple bullets to be fired without the need to reload. Meet the Press used such a\nmagazine to illustrate for its viewers the issues discussed in the interview.\n\nMeet the Press briefly borrowed the empty magazine from a private citizen who lives\noutside of the District of Columbia and who Meet the Press understood possessed the magazine\nlawfully. At no time while the magazine was in the offices of Meet the Press did it contain any\nammunition or was it attached to any firearm. There was no intent to use the empty magazine\nfor any purpose other than to illustrate for viewers of Meet the Press an issue that is currently the\nsubject of substantial public debate and news coverage. The magazine was immediately\nreturned to its owner following the broadcast.\n\nPrior to the broadcast, on Friday, December 21, 2012, NBC made inquiries to law\nenforcement authorities about the permissibility of obtaining and using an unloaded magazine on\nthe broadcast for the above-described editorial purposes. NBC contacted the Office of Public\nand Governmental Affairs at the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (\u201cATF\u201d)\nand was advised that the use of an unloaded magazine would be permissible. We understand that\nthe ATF official had consulted with the District of Columbia Metropolitan Police Department\n(\u201cMPD\u201d) before providing this advice.\n\nAlso on December 21, NBC made an inquiry to the Office of Communications of MPD.\n\nThe MPD advised that DC law prohibits the possession of a high capacity magazine and\nsuggested the program instead use a photograph.\n\n{00585849:v1}",
